WEBVTT

00:00.500 --> 00:04.500
Some beams have more supports than equilibrium can pay for.

00:04.500 --> 00:11.100
This recap shows where the extra equations come from, works two examples, and checks every number twice.

00:11.800 --> 00:13.300
Part 1.

00:13.300 --> 00:14.675
Counting.

00:15.375 --> 00:19.100
A beam in a plane gives three equilibrium equations.

00:19.100 --> 00:23.850
A simply supported beam has three reactions, so it is determinate.

00:23.850 --> 00:27.225
A propped cantilever has four, one too many.

00:27.225 --> 00:30.600
So does a beam continuous over three supports.

00:30.600 --> 00:33.500
A beam fixed at both ends has six.

00:33.500 --> 00:39.125
Each surplus reaction needs one extra equation, and it comes from compatibility.

00:39.825 --> 00:41.150
Part 2.

00:41.150 --> 00:43.075
The force method.

00:43.775 --> 00:48.000
Pick one reaction as the redundant, here the force in the prop.

00:48.000 --> 00:52.700
Remove it, and the beam becomes a cantilever, which statics can solve.

00:52.700 --> 00:58.300
Find how far the tip falls under the load, and how far it rises per unit prop force.

00:58.300 --> 01:02.050
The real prop does not move, so the two must cancel.

01:02.050 --> 01:05.075
Then equilibrium gives everything else.

01:05.775 --> 01:14.775
A cantilever under a uniform load sags w L to the fourth over eight E I at the tip: here, 101.25 millimetres.

01:14.775 --> 01:18.525
A unit tip force lifts it L cubed over three E I.

01:18.525 --> 01:25.000
Setting the two equal gives the prop force: three eighths of the load, 22.5 kilonewtons.

01:25.000 --> 01:32.900
Equilibrium then gives 37.5 kilonewtons and a hogging moment of 45 kilonewton metres at the wall.

01:33.600 --> 01:38.825
Without the prop, the wall carries a moment of 180 kilonewton metres.

01:38.825 --> 01:49.025
With it, the wall moment falls to 45, and the largest sagging moment is 25.3, where the shear is zero, 3.75 metres from the wall.

01:49.025 --> 01:52.425
The moment changes sign a quarter of the way along.

01:53.125 --> 01:54.600
Part 3.

01:54.600 --> 01:56.750
Continuous beams.

01:57.450 --> 02:02.700
For a beam over several supports, take the moments over the supports as the redundants.

02:02.700 --> 02:08.400
Each span becomes simply supported, and the slopes on either side of a support must match.

02:08.400 --> 02:11.275
That gives the three moment equation.

02:11.275 --> 02:16.700
At pinned ends the moment is zero, so one interior support means one equation.

02:17.400 --> 02:22.050
Two spans, of 4 and 6 metres, carry 10 kilonewtons per metre.

02:22.050 --> 02:27.550
The three moment equation reads 20 times M B equals minus 700.

02:27.550 --> 02:32.800
So the moment over the middle support is 35 kilonewton metres, hogging.

02:32.800 --> 02:37.350
Each span, taken on its own with that end moment, gives the reactions.

02:38.050 --> 02:43.650
The moment over the support, 35 kilonewton metres, is the largest in the beam.

02:43.650 --> 02:52.525
The long span still sags by up to 29.2, and the short span, held down by the support moment, sags only 6.33.

02:53.225 --> 02:54.575
Part 4.

02:54.575 --> 02:55.625
Checks.

02:56.325 --> 02:59.575
Moment distribution gets there another way.

02:59.575 --> 03:03.775
Lock joint B, and each span starts with its fixed end moment.

03:03.775 --> 03:13.375
Release the joint, and an unbalance of 25 kilonewton metres is shared in proportion to stiffness: 15 to the short span and 10 to the long one.

03:13.375 --> 03:17.250
The end moments come out at 35, the same answer.

03:17.950 --> 03:19.875
Run four checks.

03:19.875 --> 03:22.800
The reactions must add up to the load.

03:22.800 --> 03:26.525
Equal spans must give w L squared over eight.

03:26.525 --> 03:30.000
The deflection must be zero at every support.

03:30.000 --> 03:36.575
And an independent solver must agree: the beam visualizer on this site matches every number in this talk.

03:37.275 --> 03:39.425
Remember four things.

03:39.425 --> 03:41.800
Count the unknowns first.

03:41.800 --> 03:45.525
Write one compatibility equation per redundant.

03:45.525 --> 03:48.950
For continuous beams, use the support moments.

03:48.950 --> 03:52.900
And check with a second method, not only with equilibrium.
